Here's my analysis of `InputValidatorTests.cs`:

---

## Test Maintainability Report

**Summary:** 1 high-value finding. All 9 tests follow an identical structure — they are strong candidates for parameterized tests.

---

### Finding: Copy-Paste Test Methods → Convert to `[DataTestMethod]`

All 9 methods share the exact same body shape: construct validator, call `Validate`, assert `IsValid` + error message. They differ only in the **input string**, **expected validity**, and **expected error message**.

**Two groups can be collapsed:**

#### Group A — Invalid inputs (5 methods, lines 8–87)
`Validate_EmptyString`, `Validate_TooShort`, `Validate_ContainsSpecialChars`, `Validate_Null`, `Validate_WhitespaceOnly`

#### Group B — Valid inputs (3 methods, lines 44–68)
`Validate_ValidInput`, `Validate_MinLength`, `Validate_MaxLength`

**Before (representative sample):**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_EmptyString_ReturnsFalse()
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate("");
    Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual("Input cannot be empty", result.ErrorMessage);
}

[TestMethod]
public void Validate_TooShort_ReturnsFalse()
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate("ab");
    Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual("Input must be at least 3 characters", result.ErrorMessage);
}
// ...7 more nearly identical methods
```

**After — single parameterized method:**
```csharp
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow("",        false, "Input cannot be empty",                DisplayName = "Empty string")]
[DataRow(null,      false, "Input cannot be empty",                DisplayName = "Null")]
[DataRow("   ",     false, "Input cannot be empty",                DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
[DataRow("ab",      false, "Input must be at least 3 characters",  DisplayName = "Too short")]
[DataRow("hello<script>", false, "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
[DataRow("abc",     true,  null,                                   DisplayName = "Min length")]
[DataRow("hello-world", true, null,                                DisplayName = "Valid input")]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpectedResult(string? input, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
}
```

> **Note:** The "too long" case (`new string('x', 256)`) can't be a `[DataRow]` since it's not a compile-time constant. Add it as a separate `[TestMethod]`, or use `[DynamicData]` if you prefer everything in one method.

**Impact:** 9 methods (~80 lines) → 1–2 methods (~20 lines). Adding a new test case becomes a single `[DataRow]` line.

---

### Trade-offs

| Gain | Cost |
|------|------|
| Adding a case = one `[DataRow]` line | Individual test names are now in `DisplayName` strings instead of method names |
| Far less scrolling / noise | A failing row requires reading the `DisplayName` to identify which case broke |
| Single assertion logic to maintain | Slightly harder to set a debugger breakpoint on one specific case |

### Not flagged

- **`new InputValidator()` in every test** — it's a trivial no-arg constructor, so extracting a factory or `[TestInitialize]` isn't worth the indirection.

---

**Bottom line:** Convert to `[DataTestMethod]`/`[DataRow]`. Each new validation rule becomes a one-liner instead of a 7-line method. Would you like me to apply this refactoring?
